Locking: SDD

Purpose:
  Define resource locking model for the .sdd system — how exclusive
  and shared access to resources is managed, how deadlocks are prevented,
  and how AI task collisions are avoided. Reusable system intelligence;
  concrete lock state lives in projects/{project_name}/dependencies/locks.sdd.

Owns:
  Lock types, acquisition/release rules, deadlock detection,
  lease/timeout model, lock recovery, AI task collision prevention.

LockTypes:
  EXCLUSIVE: only one holder; blocks all others; use for mutations, writes, deployments
  SHARED: multiple holders; blocks exclusive requests; use for reads, queries
  OPTIMISTIC: no upfront lock; conflict detected at commit; use for low-contention resources
  LEASE: time-limited exclusive; auto-released after expiry; use for long-running tasks
  ADVISORY: voluntary coordination; signals intent; use for planned maintenance

LockStates:
  PENDING: lock requested, waiting
  ACQUIRED: lock held
  RELEASED: lock was held, now free
  EXPIRED: lease timed out
  CONFLICT: acquisition blocked by existing lock
  DEADLOCK: lock cycle detected

LockAcquisition:
  1. Check existing locks on resource
  2. Check compatibility
  3. Check for deadlock potential
  4. If compatible → ACQUIRED
  5. If conflict → CONFLICT (queue or reject)
  6. If deadlock → DEADLOCK (resolve)

LockingModes:
  Pessimistic:
    Lock acquired before access. Blocks until acquired or timeout.
    Use when: high contention, conflict cost is high, critical path resource
    Rules:
      [LK1] EXCLUSIVE lock MUST be acquired before mutation.
      [LK2] SHARED lock MUST be acquired before read if consistency required.
      [LK3] Lock timeout MUST be defined for all blocking acquisitions.
      [LK4] Failed acquisition MUST NOT block indefinitely.

  Optimistic:
    No lock acquired. Conflict detected at commit.
    Use when: low contention, conflict cost is low, human-edited content
    Rules:
      [LK5] Optimistic access MUST include version/timestamp check at commit.
      [LK6] Conflict MUST trigger retry or escalation, not silent overwrite.

  Lease:
    Time-limited exclusive lock. Auto-released after expiry.
    Lifecycle: ACQUIRE → (active) → RENEW | EXPIRE → RELEASE
    Rules:
      [LK7] LEASE lock MUST have explicit duration.
      [LK8] LEASE lock MAY be renewed if renew = true.
      [LK9] Expired LEASE MUST be automatically released.
      [LK10] Task using LEASE MUST handle expiration.

DeadlockDetection:
  Cycle in lock wait graph = deadlock.
  Detection: on each lock request, build wait-for graph and run cycle detection.
  Prevention:
    [LK11] Lock ordering: resources MUST be acquired in canonical order.
    [LK12] Timeout: lock acquisition MUST timeout after max_wait.
    [LK13] Deadlock detection MUST run on every lock request.
  Resolution:
    [LK14] On deadlock: abort youngest transaction (shortest hold time).
    [LK15] Aborted task MUST release all held locks.
    [LK16] Deadlock event MUST be logged with full graph.

LockTimeout:
  All blocking acquisitions MUST have timeout.
  on_timeout: WAIT | RETRY | ESCALATE | ABORT
  Rules:
    [LK17] Timeout MUST be shorter than dependent task's deadline.
    [LK18] Retry after timeout MUST use backoff.
    [LK19] Repeated timeout SHOULD trigger escalation to human.

LockRecovery:
  When lock holder crashes or becomes unresponsive:
  1. Detect: heartbeat timeout or explicit failure
  2. Classify: LEASE (auto-expire) vs PERSISTENT (manual recovery)
  3. Recover:
     LEASE → wait for expiry or force-release after safety margin
     PERSISTENT → escalate to human or use recovery policy
  4. Notify: inform waiters of lock status change
  Rules:
    [LK20] LEASE recovery MUST NOT release before safety margin.
    [LK21] PERSISTENT lock recovery MUST require human approval unless policy defines automatic recovery.
    [LK22] Lock recovery MUST notify all waiting requesters.

AITaskCollisionPrevention:
  [LK23] AI agent MUST acquire lock before starting task work.
  [LK24] Lock request includes agent_id and task_id.
  [LK25] Second agent requesting same task/resource MUST wait or select alternative.
  [LK26] Lock release MUST happen on task state transition to DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.
  [LK27] Detected collision → second agent receives TASK_LOCKED event.
  [LK28] Second agent selects next candidate from scheduler.
  [LK29] Human MAY override lock to reassign task.

LockGovernance:
  [LK30] Lock acquisition MUST go through policy engine.
  [LK31] Human-requested locks have priority over AI-requested locks.
  [LK32] System locks (maintenance, backup) have highest priority.
  [LK33] Lock hold time SHOULD be minimized — acquire late, release early.
  [LK34] Lock audit log MUST record all acquisitions, releases, timeouts, conflicts, and deadlocks.

